<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice Day</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Remembrance Day, Veterans Day</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice</a> Day is commemorated every year on 11 November to mark the armistice signed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at Compiègne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which took effect at 11:00 am on that day in 1918 and ended hostilities on the Western Front. The first official Armistice Day events were held in the grounds of Buckingham Palace on the morning of 11 November 1919 and included a two-minute silence for those who died in the war. Around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many countries changed the holiday's name and several widened it to all veterans: most Commonwealth states adopted the name Remembrance Day, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> changed it in 1954. The date is a national holiday in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-day.jpg" alt="Armistice Day" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armistice_Day,_Wall_Street.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
